---
permalink: /admin/analytics/
title: "Analytics | Admin"
bodyScripts:
  - src: "/scripts/admin-analytics.js"
    type: "module"
  - src: "/scripts/contact-widget.js"
    type: "module"
  - src: "/scripts/palette.js"
    type: "module"
---
<section class="app-page">
  <div class="an-wrap">

  {% include "chrome/admin-sections.njk" %}

  {# A dashboard, not a document: the page takes the full width like the other admin grids, and
     what used to be a paragraph per figure is a one-line description here plus the notes at the
     bottom. Every rule the old layout stated still holds and is enforced in admin-analytics.js:
     an unmeasured figure is a dash, an empty window says so in words, every chart has a table. #}
  <header class="mb-6 flex flex-wrap items-end justify-between gap-4">
    <div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-text">Analytics</h1>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-brand-muted">
        Both properties, counted on my own server. No cookie, no address, no User-Agent string.
      </p>
    </div>

    {# Two controls, and everything below is drawn from the one answer they fetch together - so
       nothing on this page can be showing a different window, or a different property, from
       anything else on it. The property filter is a real parameter rather than a filter applied
       here: a visit that crossed from the site to the app is two visits in two properties, so a
       total filtered in the browser would be right for the lists and wrong for every rate. #}
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
      {# Right now: the last five minutes, whatever the window says. #}
      <span class="an-live" title="The last five minutes, whatever the window says.">
        <span class="an-live-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span id="live" data-loading>—</span>
        <span>now</span>
        <span id="liveNote" class="sr-only">The last five minutes, whatever the window says.</span>
      </span>
      <label for="range" class="sr-only">Window</label>
      <select id="range" class="field w-auto py-1.5 text-sm">
        <option value="7">Last 7 days</option>
        <option value="30" selected>Last 30 days</option>
        <option value="90">Last 90 days</option>
        <option value="365">Last 365 days</option>
      </select>
      <label for="property" class="sr-only">Site</label>
      <select id="property" class="field w-auto py-1.5 text-sm">
        <option value="all" selected>Both sites</option>
        <option value="site">Public site</option>
        <option value="app">App</option>
      </select>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div id="status" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="mb-4 text-sm"></div>

  {# The headline strip. Every figure starts as an em dash marked `data-loading`, never as a
     zero: a zero is a measurement and these have not been measured yet. Visitors and Views are
     buttons because they choose what the chart below draws; the rest are figures only. #}
  <div class="card an-kpis mb-4">
    <button type="button" class="an-kpi" data-metric="visitors" aria-pressed="true">
      <span class="an-kpi-label">Unique visitors</span>
      <span class="an-kpi-number" id="visitors" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="an-kpi-note" id="visitorsNote">Browsers, counted once a day each.</span>
    </button>
    <div class="an-kpi">
      <span class="an-kpi-label">Visits</span>
      <span class="an-kpi-number" id="visits" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="an-kpi-note" id="visitsNote">A gap of 30 minutes starts a new one.</span>
    </div>
    <button type="button" class="an-kpi" data-metric="views" aria-pressed="false">
      <span class="an-kpi-label">Total views</span>
      <span class="an-kpi-number" id="views" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="an-kpi-note" id="viewsNote">In this window.</span>
    </button>
    <div class="an-kpi">
      <span class="an-kpi-label">Views per visit</span>
      <span class="an-kpi-number" id="perVisit" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="an-kpi-note" id="perVisitNote">Pages read before leaving.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="an-kpi">
      <span class="an-kpi-label">Bounce rate</span>
      <span class="an-kpi-number" id="bounce" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="an-kpi-note" id="bounceNote">Visits that read one page.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="an-kpi">
      <span class="an-kpi-label">Average visit</span>
      <span class="an-kpi-number" id="duration" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="an-kpi-note" id="durationNote">Between the first and last page.</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="chart" class="card mb-4">
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2 mb-4">
    <section class="card an-panel">
      <div class="an-panel-head">
        <h2 class="an-panel-title">Top sources</h2>
        <p class="text-xs text-brand-muted">
          From LinkedIn: <strong id="linkedin" class="text-brand-text" data-loading>—</strong>
          <span id="linkedinNote" class="sr-only">Views whose referrer was a LinkedIn domain.</span>
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="an-panel-hint">The host only; the rest of a referring URL is dropped before anything is written.</p>
      <div id="referrers"><p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p></div>
    </section>

    <section class="card an-panel">
      <div class="an-panel-head">
        <h2 class="an-panel-title">Pages</h2>
        <div class="an-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Pages">
          <button type="button" role="tab" class="an-tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="paths" id="tab-paths">Top pages</button>
          <button type="button" role="tab" class="an-tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="journeys" id="tab-journeys" tabindex="-1">Entry &amp; exit</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div id="paths" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-paths"><p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p></div>
      <div id="journeys" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-journeys" hidden><p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p></div>
    </section>

    <section class="card an-panel">
      <div class="an-panel-head">
        <h2 class="an-panel-title">Countries</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="an-panel-hint">Two letters looked up from the address, which is then discarded. Since 26 September 2026.</p>
      <div id="countries"><p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p></div>
    </section>

    <section class="card an-panel">
      <div class="an-panel-head">
        <h2 class="an-panel-title">Devices</h2>
      </div>
      <div id="agents"><p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p></div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section class="card mb-4">
    <div class="an-panel-head">
      <h2 class="an-panel-title">When it is read</h2>
      <p class="text-xs text-brand-muted">Day against hour, in UTC</p>
    </div>
    <div id="heatmap" class="an-heat mt-3"><p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <div class="an-panel-head">
      <h2 class="an-panel-title">The last twenty visits</h2>
      <p class="text-xs text-brand-muted">No visitor number: what a visit read, not who</p>
    </div>
    <div id="recent" class="mt-3"><p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p></div>
  </section>

  {# The five things about these numbers that are artefacts of how they are made rather than
     facts about readers. Written on the page and not only in the code, because a dashboard that
     prints "average visit 41s" beside "bounce 62%" without saying the second one produced the
     first is a dashboard that misleads its only reader. #}
  <details class="card mt-6">
    <summary class="cursor-pointer text-sm font-semibold text-brand-text">
      What these figures cannot tell you
    </summary>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-brand-muted">
      <li><strong class="text-brand-text">A bounce lasts zero seconds, by arithmetic.</strong>
        The clock runs between two page views, so the last page of every visit is untimed and a
        one-page visit is untimed entirely. Every bounce therefore averages in as nought, which is
        most of why the average visit looks short. Read the two figures together or neither.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-brand-text">A visit that began before the window is cut at its
        edge.</strong> Its earlier pages are outside the range and the query cannot see them, so
        the first visit of any window reads shorter and thinner than it was.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-brand-text">The same reader on both sites is two visitors.</strong>
        The number is computed per property on purpose - two sites, two consent regimes - so
        signing in from the public site is counted as arriving somewhere new. That is a choice,
        not a measurement.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-brand-text">"Visitors" means browsers.</strong> One person on a
        phone and a laptop is two, and the same browser tomorrow is two more. Every analytics
        product means this by the word; none of them says so.</li>
      <li id="unidentifiedNote"></li>
    </ul>
  </details>


  </div>
</section>
